From adfb4ec0edfdae732ff3046924fc0de490bff7eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Oct 2025 07:15:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Add trust-based permission system for sub-agent control
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Implements a sophisticated permission management system that sets cc2cc
apart from other agent frameworks by establishing a proper trust hierarchy:
User → Main Agent → Sub-agents

KEY FEATURES:
- Sub-agents request permission instead of running with full access
- Automatic policy evaluation (auto-approve/deny/escalate)
- Risk assessment based on action type and details
- User escalation for medium-risk operations
- Customizable permission policies

COMPONENTS:
- permissions.py: Core permission models and manager
  - PermissionRequest: Request data model
  - PermissionPolicy: Configurable approval policies
  - PermissionManager: Policy evaluation and decision engine

- MCP Tools (5 new):
  - request_permission: Sub-agents request approval
  - get_pending_permissions: View requests needing review
  - approve_permission: Approve a request
  - deny_permission: Deny with reason
  - get_permission_status: Check request status

BUILT-IN POLICIES:
✅ Auto-approve: Safe reads, git status/log/diff, standard package installs
✗ Auto-deny: Destructive git ops (push --force, reset --hard), dangerous commands
⚠️ Escalate: File deletions, network requests, sensitive file access

TESTING:
- 15 new unit tests for permission system
- All 68 tests passing
- Comprehensive coverage of policy matching, risk assessment, escalation

This trust-based approach means:
- Sub-agents work autonomously on safe operations
- Risky actions always have oversight
- Users maintain control without micromanaging
- Agents are all about trust - cc2cc makes it explicit

